                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.2531/63: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Chile</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Culbertson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                            State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Santiago</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-06-03T14:00:00-04:00">June 3, 1933—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 3—2 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>76. Today in a lengthy note replying to my recent communications regarding
                    American frozen credits, the Foreign Office states that if the principal nations
                    at the London Conference agree to the elimination of compensation agreements
                    Chile will gladly obligate itself to take a similar course. Pending the outcome
                    of this Conference the note proposes the postponement of any negotiations
                    looking to a compensation agreement but at the same time expresses a willingness
                    to continue a study of the problem and to receive any proposal which we may wish
                    to offer. The tone of the note invites affirmative action.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Culbertson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
